1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
Last time, every atom of every monomer ended

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:04,640
up in the chain.

3
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:06,800
A PET drink bottle is different.

4
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:09,280
Every time two of its monomers join, a

5
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,760
small molecule of water comes off.

6
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:14,320
Nylon rope is made the same way.

7
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:16,640
So how can you build a long chain

8
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:19,360
while losing something at every link, and what

9
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:21,200
do those links do to the plastic?

10
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,960
Remember esterification.

11
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:28,320
An acid and an alcohol join, and lose

12
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:28,880
water.

13
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:32,160
But ethyl ethanoate stops there, because it has

14
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:35,120
no spare alcohol or carboxyl group to keep

15
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:36,240
building this chain.

16
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:39,440
Now give each molecule a reactive group at

17
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:42,320
both ends: a diol, with two O H

18
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:45,760
groups, and a dicarboxylic acid, with two carboxyl

19
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:47,000
groups.

20
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,760
After one link forms, the new molecule still

21
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:51,840
has a reactive group at each end, so

22
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:55,120
it can keep joining, in both directions, thousands

23
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:56,000
of times.

24
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,600
That's how PET is made.

25
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:02,880
Its monomers are ethane one two diol, and

26
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:06,800
benzene one four dicarboxylic acid, a benzene ring

27
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:09,200
with a carboxyl group on opposite sides.

28
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:12,320
At each link, the acid loses O H

29
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:14,160
and the alcohol loses H.

30
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:16,640
They leave as water, and an ester link

31
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:17,200
forms.

32
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,080
Building a polymer this way, losing a small

33
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:24,480
molecule at every link, is called condensation polymerisation.

34
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,080
With ester links all along its chain, PET

35
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:29,440
is a polyester.

36
00:01:31,467 --> 00:01:34,186
To draw the repeat unit, take one diol

37
00:01:34,347 --> 00:01:37,226
and one acid, joined, with the water gone

38
00:01:37,307 --> 00:01:38,106
from each end.

39
00:01:38,747 --> 00:01:42,026
Start at the diol's oxygen: O, C H

40
00:01:42,107 --> 00:01:43,946
two, C H two, O,

41
00:01:44,507 --> 00:01:46,906
then the acid's C double bond O, the

42
00:01:46,987 --> 00:01:49,306
ring, and the other C double bond O.

43
00:01:49,867 --> 00:01:52,506
Put brackets around it, with a continuation bond

44
00:01:52,587 --> 00:01:54,346
out of the oxygen on the left and

45
00:01:54,427 --> 00:01:56,506
the carbon on the right, and an n

46
00:01:56,587 --> 00:01:57,586
outside.

47
00:01:57,947 --> 00:02:00,186
Check the ends: the carbon on the right

48
00:02:00,267 --> 00:02:02,186
can bond to an oxygen just like the

49
00:02:02,267 --> 00:02:04,346
one on the left, so the units join.

50
00:02:04,907 --> 00:02:07,706
And notice each repeat unit holds two ester

51
00:02:07,907 --> 00:02:08,346
links.

52
00:02:10,367 --> 00:02:13,006
Now swap the diol for a diamine, a

53
00:02:13,087 --> 00:02:15,086
molecule with an N H two group at

54
00:02:15,167 --> 00:02:15,926
each end.

55
00:02:16,207 --> 00:02:20,366
Hexane one six diamine reacts with hexanedioic acid.

56
00:02:20,607 --> 00:02:23,246
This time the amine group loses an H,

57
00:02:23,287 --> 00:02:25,806
the acid loses O H, and water leaves

58
00:02:25,887 --> 00:02:26,446
again.

59
00:02:26,687 --> 00:02:29,006
The new link is an amide link, a

60
00:02:29,087 --> 00:02:31,406
C double bond O bonded to N H,

61
00:02:31,487 --> 00:02:32,926
as you saw with amides.

62
00:02:33,167 --> 00:02:36,526
So this polymer is a polyamide, nylon six

63
00:02:36,727 --> 00:02:39,006
six, named for the six carbons in each

64
00:02:39,087 --> 00:02:39,726
monomer.

65
00:02:39,967 --> 00:02:42,846
Its repeat unit runs N H, six C

66
00:02:42,927 --> 00:02:45,886
H two groups, N H, then C double

67
00:02:45,967 --> 00:02:48,606
bond O, four C H two groups, and

68
00:02:48,687 --> 00:02:49,886
C double bond O.

69
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:54,660
Going backwards, you add the water back.

70
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:57,820
Find each ester or amide link, and cut

71
00:02:57,900 --> 00:02:59,980
it between the C double bond O carbon

72
00:03:00,140 --> 00:03:01,660
and the O or N.

73
00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:03,820
Add O H to the C double bond

74
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:06,380
O side, and H to the O or

75
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:07,340
N side.

76
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:09,260
Take this polyester.

77
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:12,140
Cutting gives ethane one two diol, and an

78
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:13,100
acid.

79
00:03:13,180 --> 00:03:16,140
Now count the acid's carbons, including both C

80
00:03:16,220 --> 00:03:18,540
double bond O carbons, not just the C

81
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:19,820
H twos.

82
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:23,180
That's six, so it's hexanedioic acid.

83
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:27,400
So how do the two families compare?

84
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:30,080
An addition monomer needs a C double bond

85
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:33,200
C, and a condensation monomer needs a reactive

86
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:34,720
group at each end.

87
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:37,600
Addition releases nothing, so the repeat unit has

88
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:39,920
the same formula as its monomer.

89
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:43,280
Condensation loses a small molecule at every link,

90
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:44,720
here water.

91
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:47,760
For PET and nylon, made from these pairs,

92
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:49,920
the repeat unit is one of each monomer

93
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,840
minus two waters.

94
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:53,680
And look at the chain itself.

95
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:57,280
Addition gives an all carbon backbone, while condensation

96
00:03:57,440 --> 00:03:59,760
puts ester or amide links in it.

97
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:02,320
Water can split those links again, the reverse

98
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:04,400
reaction, called hydrolysis.

99
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:07,360
It's usually slow without acid, base or an

100
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:10,160
enzyme, but a carbon backbone has no such

101
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:10,960
weak point.

102
00:04:12,967 --> 00:04:14,366
Now the properties.

103
00:04:14,647 --> 00:04:18,566
Polyethylene's chains are held only by dispersion forces.

104
00:04:18,807 --> 00:04:21,046
But nylon has an N H and a

105
00:04:21,167 --> 00:04:24,406
C double bond O in every amide link.

106
00:04:24,487 --> 00:04:26,326
The N H on one chain forms a

107
00:04:26,407 --> 00:04:28,726
hydrogen bond with the C double bond O

108
00:04:28,767 --> 00:04:31,686
on the next chain, all along their length.

109
00:04:31,807 --> 00:04:34,406
Those hydrogen bonds hold the chains together much

110
00:04:34,487 --> 00:04:37,606
more strongly, so nylon is tough, and it

111
00:04:37,687 --> 00:04:39,526
melts at about two hundred and sixty five

112
00:04:39,607 --> 00:04:43,686
degrees, far above HDPE's hundred and thirty.

113
00:04:43,807 --> 00:04:46,566
That suits strong ropes and fishing line.

114
00:04:46,647 --> 00:04:48,966
Heat it enough, and the chains slide, so

115
00:04:49,007 --> 00:04:50,806
it can still be remoulded.

116
00:04:50,887 --> 00:04:53,846
The amide links inside each chain don't break.

117
00:04:55,867 --> 00:04:58,586
PET's repeat unit has no O H or

118
00:04:58,747 --> 00:05:01,626
N H to donate hydrogen bonds, so these

119
00:05:01,707 --> 00:05:03,786
chains don't hydrogen bond to each other.

120
00:05:04,507 --> 00:05:07,386
But its benzene rings are flat and stiff,

121
00:05:07,467 --> 00:05:10,346
so the chains can't twist and bend easily,

122
00:05:10,387 --> 00:05:13,066
and its polar ester links add dipole dipole

123
00:05:13,147 --> 00:05:14,666
attraction between chains.

124
00:05:15,307 --> 00:05:18,506
So PET is strong and stiff, and it

125
00:05:18,587 --> 00:05:20,906
melts at about two hundred and sixty degrees.

126
00:05:21,547 --> 00:05:24,266
Blown into a bottle, it's tough and clear.

127
00:05:24,907 --> 00:05:27,866
Spun into thin fibres, the same polymer becomes

128
00:05:27,947 --> 00:05:29,226
polyester clothing.

129
00:05:31,234 --> 00:05:33,953
So, addition: nothing lost.

130
00:05:34,194 --> 00:05:37,233
Condensation: a small molecule lost.

131
00:05:37,554 --> 00:05:40,353
To draw a condensation polymer, take one of

132
00:05:40,434 --> 00:05:43,313
each monomer, remove the water at each link,

133
00:05:43,354 --> 00:05:44,913
and bracket the rest.

134
00:05:44,994 --> 00:05:47,793
To find the monomers, add the water back.

135
00:05:47,874 --> 00:05:49,953
And to explain a property, look at what

136
00:05:50,034 --> 00:05:53,153
the chains share: N H and C double

137
00:05:53,234 --> 00:05:56,193
bond O pairs give hydrogen bonds, and rings

138
00:05:56,354 --> 00:05:57,713
make stiff chains.

139
00:05:59,734 --> 00:06:00,373
Your turn.

140
00:06:00,934 --> 00:06:04,853
Butane one four diol and butanedioic acid react

141
00:06:04,934 --> 00:06:07,053
to form a condensation polymer.

142
00:06:07,334 --> 00:06:08,733
Draw the repeat unit.

143
00:06:09,014 --> 00:06:11,653
Then name the type of link formed, and

144
00:06:11,734 --> 00:06:12,893
the other product.

145
00:06:13,174 --> 00:06:14,453
Pause here to answer.

146
00:06:25,667 --> 00:06:28,226
Join the diol's oxygen to the acid's C

147
00:06:28,347 --> 00:06:31,066
double bond O carbon, and water leaves.

148
00:06:31,347 --> 00:06:33,906
So the repeat unit is O, four C

149
00:06:33,987 --> 00:06:37,346
H twos, O, C double bond O, two

150
00:06:37,507 --> 00:06:40,146
C H twos, C double bond O, in

151
00:06:40,227 --> 00:06:41,346
brackets with n.

152
00:06:41,987 --> 00:06:44,146
The links are ester links, and the other

153
00:06:44,227 --> 00:06:45,426
product is water.

154
00:06:46,067 --> 00:06:47,706
Leaving an O H at the end of

155
00:06:47,747 --> 00:06:49,106
the bracket is the trap.

156
00:06:49,507 --> 00:06:51,586
That water has already gone.

157
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:54,240
Last one.

158
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:57,280
A polyamide has the repeat unit shown.

159
00:06:57,840 --> 00:07:00,480
Identify the two monomers used to make it.

160
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:03,520
Then explain why it melts at a much

161
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:06,720
higher temperature than HDPE, in terms of the

162
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:08,680
forces between the chains.

163
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:10,240
Pause here to answer.

164
00:07:21,467 --> 00:07:24,106
Add the water back at each amide link:

165
00:07:24,187 --> 00:07:26,146
H on the N, O H on the

166
00:07:26,187 --> 00:07:27,306
C double bond O.

167
00:07:27,787 --> 00:07:31,386
That gives hexane one six diamine, and decanedioic

168
00:07:31,467 --> 00:07:34,346
acid, with ten carbons, counting both C double

169
00:07:34,427 --> 00:07:35,946
bond O carbons.

170
00:07:36,067 --> 00:07:38,586
Its chains hydrogen bond, N H to C

171
00:07:38,667 --> 00:07:41,466
double bond O, on top of dispersion, while

172
00:07:41,547 --> 00:07:44,986
HDPE has only dispersion forces.

173
00:07:45,067 --> 00:07:47,466
Octanedioic acid is the trap.

174
00:07:47,547 --> 00:07:49,466
It leaves out those two carbons.

175
